Summary of Changes to multiemotibit_UDP_SD_RFv2.py
Session Date: May 28, 2026
Issue 1: Missing Data in CSV Files ✓ FIXED
Problem:

CSV files were only saving raw EmotiBit sensor data
Valence, arousal, SD metrics, and processing time were calculated but not being saved
Solution:
All calculated metrics (valence, arousal, edl_sd, temperature_roc_sd, scr_frequency_sd, hr_sd, ibi_sd, processing_time_ms) now properly saved to CSV files for offline analysis

Issue 2: Terminal Output Overhead ✓ OPTIMIZED
Problem: Script was printing metrics every second to terminal
Terminal I/O can add significant CPU overhead, especially with multiple devices
Solution:

Implemented print throttling:
First 3 seconds: Print every second (confirm system working)
After 3 seconds: Print once every 30 seconds only
All data still saved to CSV and published to Redis every second
Result:
Reduced system overhead while maintaining monitoring capability

What's Sent to Redis
3 separate channels, published every 1 second:

Channel 1: device:{device_id}:physio_metrics
Contains: 5 SD metrics in one message
{
  "device": "MD-V5-0001019",
  "timestamp": "2026-05-28T14:30:45.123456",
  "edl_sd": 0.123,
  "temperature_roc_sd": 0.045,
  "scr_frequency_sd": 0.234,
  "hr_sd": 2.567,
  "ibi_sd": 15.789
}

Channel 2: device:{device_id}:valence_cont
Contains: Valence prediction
{
  "device": "MD-V5-0001019",
  "valence": 1.45,
  "timestamp": "2026-05-28T14:30:45.123456"
}

Channel 3: device:{device_id}:arousal_cont
Contains: Arousal prediction
{
  "device": "MD-V5-0001019",
  "arousal": 1.78,
  "timestamp": "2026-05-28T14:30:45.123456"
}

What's NOT Sent to Redis
❌ Raw sensor data (too much data)
❌ Processing time (saved to CSV only)
❌ Individual sample values

Total: 3 messages per second per device - lightweight and efficient for real-time streaming.
